Acute Urinary Retention
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Inability to pass urine, Severe suprapubic pain, Palpable dull suprapubic mass, Bladder volume >400mL

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-004
Reason for Visit: Agonizing lower abdominal pain and absolute inability to void, requiring emergency assessment and immediate mechanical relief of bladder obstruction.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly male in extreme physical distress, thrashing in bed with agonizing suprapubic pain and an absolute inability to pass urine for over 12 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to confirm acute urinary retention and perform immediate urinary decompression, as delaying care until in-hours would risk irreversible detrusor muscle damage, acute renal failure, and bladder rupture.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm bursting, the pain is unbearable, but I can't force a single drop out!"
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated rapidly over the past 12 hours. The patient describes a constant, intense urge to micturate accompanied by severe, agonizing suprapubic pain. He reports an absolute inability to pass urine voluntarily, though nursing staff noted minor overflow incontinence presenting as continuous, small-volume dribbling on his bedsheets. Extreme restlessness and severe physical distress are present. The patient has a known history of progressive lower urinary tract symptoms (hesitancy, weak stream) over the past year. Yesterday, he was started on an over-the-counter antihistamine for a rash, which likely precipitated the acute anticholinergic blockade and subsequent retention.
Functional Status: Normally mobilizes with a walker; currently unable to stand still due to severe distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Resides in a nursing facility; high risk of delirium secondary to severe pain in an elderly patient.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.9C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, severely agitated, tachycardic due to sympathetic nervous system activation from severe pain, clutching lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Palpable, extremely tense, and dull-to-percussion suprapubic mass extending almost to the umbilicus, exquisitely tender to palpation. Genitourinary: Enlarged, smooth, benign-feeling prostate on digital rectal examination with no focal nodules. Expected investigation results: Bedside bladder ultrasound demonstrates a massive bladder volume exceeding 800 mL, confirming the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Wet bedsheets consistent with overflow incontinence. Antihistamine packaging visible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Diphenhydramine identified as the offending anticholinergic agent. Routine tamsulosin was missed for the last two days.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Urinary Retention - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Prostatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, rigors, and a non-tender prostate on digital rectal examination.

Constipation/Faecal Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to an empty rectal vault on examination and the explicit dull suprapubic mass specific to the bladder.

Lower Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute lack of voiding and massive bladder volume, rather than small frequent voids.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in extremis with classic signs of acute urinary retention, driven by an acute-on-chronic prostatic obstruction exacerbated by an anticholinergic medication. The objective findings of a tense, dull suprapubic mass and reactive hypertension/tachycardia secondary to severe pain mandated immediate clinical intervention. Prolonged distension risks ischemic damage to the bladder wall and retrograde acute kidney injury. An urgent after-hours visit was absolutely necessary to definitively diagnose the mechanical obstruction and provide immediate relief via urethral catheterization, as waiting for a standard daytime appointment would constitute clinical negligence and guarantee severe complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate insertion of a 16 Fr Foley indwelling urinary catheter. 850 mL of clear, non-malodorous urine drained immediately with profound, instant relief of pain and normalization of vital signs. Catheter left on continuous free drainage. Diphenhydramine ceased immediately. Tamsulosin restarted.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain catheter patency and prevent encrustation, the patient is instructed to drink a standardized glass of water with each meal. Ensuring the collection bag fills with pale, clear urine provides positive visual feedback that hydration is adequate, reinforcing the habit of regular fluid intake.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Facility nursing staff instructed on catheter care and monitoring for post-obstructive diuresis.
DME/Safety (Optional): Leg bag and overnight drainage bag provided.
Follow-up: Trial of void to be arranged in 7-10 days under urology or GP supervision.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review or ED transfer is required if the catheter stops draining entirely, if blood or thick pus appears in the tubing, or if the patient develops a high fever or confusion indicating a catheter-associated infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The catheter is a temporary measure to relieve the emergency; formal urological assessment is required for definitive management.
Clinical Course: The pain is resolved. Mild cramping or bladder spasms may occur as the stretched bladder decompresses. Post-obstructive diuresis may cause higher than normal urine output for the next 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your catheter bag stops filling, or if you experience sudden severe lower abdominal pain again, inform the nursing staff immediately so the catheter can be flushed or replaced.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bladder was blocked by your enlarged prostate and the allergy medication, causing it to stretch painfully. The tube bypasses the blockage, allowing the urine to drain continuously and letting your bladder rest.
Actionable Steps: Keep the drainage bag below the level of your bladder at all times to prevent backflow. Drink water regularly to keep the urine clear and prevent blockages.

